.TH TERMCAP 6
.SH NAME
termcap \- terminal capabilities for APE programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /sys/lib/termcap
.SH DESCRIPTION
An APE program that draws on a terminal, such as
.IR vim ,
learns how by BSD's
.I termcap
library,
.BR -ltermcap ,
whose functions read this file.
.I Tgetent
finds the entry for a name,
usually
.BR $TERM ;
.IR tgetnum ,
.I tgetflag
and
.I tgetstr
read its capabilities;
.I tgoto
puts a row and column in a cursor motion string;
and
.I tputs
writes a string.
.PP
An entry is one logical line.
A backslash before a newline joins the next line to it,
and the blanks that begin the next line are dropped.
A line that starts with
.L #
is a comment.
Fields are separated by colons.
The first field holds the entry's names, separated by
.LR | ,
and the others are its capabilities, each two characters long:
.TP
.IB xx
a flag, which the terminal has;
.TP
.IB xx # n
a number, decimal, or octal if it starts with 0;
.TP
.IB xx = s
a string, where
.L \eE
is an escape,
.BI ^ x
is control-\fIx\fP,
.L ^?
is DEL,
.LR \en ,
.LR \er ,
.LR \et ,
.LR \eb ,
.L \ef
and
.L \es
are newline, return, tab, backspace, form feed and space,
.BI \e ooo
is an octal byte,
.L \e0
being 0200, which a terminal takes as a NUL,
and a backslash before any other character is that character, so that
.L \e:
is a colon;
.TP
.IB xx @
a cancellation: the capability is absent, even if a later field gives it.
.PP
The first field for a capability is the one that counts.
In a string given to
.IR tgoto ,
.L %d
is the next value in decimal,
.L %2
and
.L %3
the same in two or three digits,
.L %.
the value as a byte,
.BI %+ x
the value plus
.I x
as a byte,
.BI %> xy
adds
.I y
to the value if it is greater than
.IR x ,
.L %r
gives the column first,
.L %i
adds one to both,
.L %n
exclusive-ors both with 0140,
.L %B
converts the value to BCD,
.L %D
is the Delta Data encoding,
and
.L %%
is a percent sign.
The row comes first, unless
.L %r
says otherwise.
A
.L %.
or
.BI %+ x
that would send a NUL, control-D or newline sends the next byte instead
and appends a move back:
.B UP
for a row, if the program has set it, and
.B BC
for a column, a backspace if the program has not set it.
.I Tputs
drops a string's leading padding, a number and an optional
.LR * ,
and sends the rest.
.PP
.I Tgetent
returns 1 when it finds the entry, 0 when it finds none, and \-1
when it cannot read the file.
If
.B $TERMCAP
starts with
.LR / ,
it is the name of the file to read, in place of
.BR /sys/lib/termcap .
Otherwise, if it is an entry with the name asked for,
that entry is used without reading any file.
Entries cannot include others: there is no
.LR tc= .
.I Tgetnum
of
.B co
and
.B li
returns
.B $COLUMNS
and
.BR $LINES ,
when they are set, in place of the entry's.
.SS The entry
The file has one entry,
.IR vt (1)'s,
named for each
.B $TERM
.I vt
sets,
.BR xterm-256color ,
.BR xterm ,
.B vt220
and
.BR ansi ,
and for
.BR vt .
It has only what
.I vt
does: automatic margins with xterm's deferred wrap
.RB ( am ,
.BR xn ),
erasure in the current background color
.RB ( ut ),
cursor motion, scrolling regions, line and character insertion and deletion,
tab stops, bold, reverse, invisible and underlined text,
DEC's line drawing set,
256 colors
.RB ( Co ,
.BR AF ,
.BR AB ),
the alternate screen
.RB ( ti ,
.BR te ),
cursor hiding, application cursor keys
.RB ( ks ,
.BR ke ),
and the keys
.I vt
sends.
It leaves out faint, italic and blinking text, which
.I vt
does not draw.
The mouse has no capability:
.IR vim 's
.B $VIM/vimrc
sets
.B ttymouse=sgr
for it.
.SH FILES
.TF /sys/src/ape/lib/termcap
.TP
.B /sys/lib/termcap
the entries
.TP
.B /sys/include/ape/termcap.h
the library's declarations
.TP
.B /sys/src/ape/lib/termcap
the library
